# Retirement suggestions — for the owner — {{ project_name }}

Requirements an agent thinks the owner may want to retire, and why. Append only.

## Nothing reads this file

Not `coverage.sh`. Not `deliver-phase.sh`. Not any gate, any workflow, or any
other role's prompt. **The owner reads it, and nobody else.** A test in the
template asserts exactly that: if any script or command file ever starts reading
this path, CI fails.

That is deliberate, and it is the whole design. A suggestion that something be
retired must never be able to:

- excuse a requirement from the coverage report,
- stop a run, refuse a phase, or fail a check,
- keep a planner from being dispatched for the requirement,
- or change any decision that has landed.

The reason is worth stating plainly, because it was the second design that was
tried and it was wrong. If a suggestion excused a requirement from the work
list, an agent could take work off its own list by suggesting the work be
dropped — the exact thing
[`DESIGN.oracle.retired.md`](../DESIGN.oracle.retired.md) exists to keep behind
the owner. And if a suggestion could stop a run, it would be a veto with extra
steps, held by the party that is supposed to keep working.

So the requirement stays live and gets planned and built as normal until the
owner says otherwise. The cost is real and accepted: work may go into something
the owner later retires. That is cheaper than an unattended run that stalls at
3am on a judgement nobody is awake to make.

## Writing one

Append. Say which id, what evidence points at it, what the requirement would be
replaced by if anything, and what has already been built against it — the owner
is deciding whether to undo work, so the size of that work is part of the
question.

    ## R<n> — <one line on why this might be wrong>

    - **Date:** YYYY-MM-DD
    - **Evidence:** ESC-<n>, BL-<n>
    - **Replaced by:** R<m>, or "(nothing — the behaviour would simply go)"
    - **Already built against it:** <plans and pull requests, or "nothing yet">
    - **Why:** ...

To act on one, the owner appends a line to
[`DESIGN.oracle.retired.md`](../DESIGN.oracle.retired.md) on a pull request they
open themselves. To reject one, they do nothing.

